<!--
    Variadic Objective-C selectors from SDK headers (`option objcsdk <dir>`).

    Issue: with the iPhoneOS SDK named, Toyota's
    `-[APMSqliteStore increaseColumn:byValue:inTable:condition:parameterValues:error:]`
    rendered `objc_msgSend$stringWithFormat:(classRef_NSString,(NSString *)"UPDATE
    %@ SET %@ = %@ + %lld WHERE %@");` while the caller stores five arguments at
    [sp]..[sp+32]. The header declares `+stringWithFormat:(NSString *)format,
    ...`, and the reader dropped the `, ...`, so the stub prototype closed after
    the format.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement (`analyzers/objc/kuna_objcsdk.rs`
    marks the encoding variadic, `kuna_objcrefs.rs (stub_prototype)` sets
    `first_var_arg_slot`), then P4 active-input trial scoring recovers the
    stack tail (`varargstackargs`, `darwinvarargs`).

    P0 assertion: `option objcsdk <directory>|off` (env-bridged and load-time,
    so set before the `load file` it applies to and cleared at the end).

    The fixture is `macho_objc_sdk_varargs.m`: a root class `Fmt` declared and
    never implemented, each send through a linker stub;
    `tests/fixtures/objc_sdk_varargs/Fmt.h` is a hand-written header (no Apple
    text) declaring `+stringWithFormat:` and `-logLevel:format:` variadic.

    Pass 1 (`objc on`, no headers) reproduces the bug: neither call has its
    format arguments.
    Pass 2 (`objc on`, `objcsdk` at the header directory) is the fix: both calls
    keep their fixed arguments and take the two stack-passed ones.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
